  BOARD-SPECIFICITY IS UNMEASURED HERE, and that is a finding rather than a
  failure. Every wrong splice was eliminated by self-encipherment before the
  bombe was asked, so this run says nothing about what the board would do with
  a legal-but-wrong splice.

  Why that asymmetry is favourable, structurally:
    * Enigma never encodes a letter to itself, so a menu built from the TRUE
      crib at the TRUE offset with the TRUE splice is *always* legal — it is
      reconstructing real plain/cipher pairs. Legality can never reject the
      truth.
    * A wrong splice re-pairs the crib against shifted ciphertext, and over 16+
      positions it almost always hits a self-encipherment. Here that killed
      28 of 28 wrong candidates for free, with no GPU at all.

  So the splice search is largely self-pruning, which is good for cost. But the
  discrimination is coming from the cipher's own constraint, NOT from the board,
  and legality prunes by coincidence rather than by correctness. A hit therefore
  still needs independent confirmation, exactly as a short-crib stop does.

